WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.190
Film Courage: Criticism makes our best work?

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.949
Lee Jessup: Absolutely!

00:00:03.949 --> 00:00:06.169
Listen, I believe in kind criticism.

00:00:06.169 --> 00:00:09.180
I don’t believe in cruelty for the sake
of cruelty.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.840
But I don’t believe we learn much from compliments.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.930
I believe they can certainly boost our ego
and make us feel good.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:23.420
But the learning happens from what we can
improve what we can do better.

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.060
That is how we grow.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:30.800
While flattery and compliments feel great,
we’re not going to walk out a better writer

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.649
tomorrow from what we learned from the compliment.

00:00:34.649 --> 00:00:40.539
We’re going to perhaps be more self-assured
what we did originally well (which is great)

00:00:40.539 --> 00:00:43.059
and I’m not discounting that.

00:00:43.059 --> 00:00:47.989
But criticism is what teaches us and challenges
us to become our best.

00:00:47.989 --> 00:00:54.179
Film Courage: What was the internal dialogue
you had when you determined that screenwriting

00:00:54.179 --> 00:00:59.171
for you…sitting down…writing a script…maybe
wasn’t what you wanted to do but you wanted

00:00:59.171 --> 00:01:01.690
to coach people, you wanted to bring out there
best?

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:06.520
Lee Jessup: For me it was really about the
development process when I realized what a

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:13.251
foolish child I was thinking that you can
write a script and then fast forward 6 or

00:01:13.251 --> 00:01:19.450
9 or 12 months and seeing it brought to the
screen and being on set and watching your

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:20.450
vision come to life.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:29.109
I understand that I came to it from a very
naive point of view, from a fantasy based

00:01:29.109 --> 00:01:35.040
point of view that wasn’t the reality of
how movies get made and what the writer’s

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.369
experience is.

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:41.140
And I ultimately realized that this wasn’t
the experience I wanted to have or that would

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.070
make me happy or fulfilled in my day-to-day
life.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:48.850
And I was lucky enough to have discovered
that at a fairly young age (at the age of

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.710
23) to say “Whoah!

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.650
That…no.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:57.429
Nothing about this is exciting or motivating
for me.”

00:01:57.429 --> 00:02:02.229
That said, I always loved story and I always
loved writers and from there I went to development

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:05.509
and realized that my heart was with the writer.

00:02:05.509 --> 00:02:08.009
The writers were my people.

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:13.560
More so than the directors (that I never really
got) or the actors (that I dated but never

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.640
really got).

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.640
My heart was with the writers and so I wanted
to foster talent.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.990
I wanted to support writers.

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:28.931
And it’s funny because today if I watch
a tennis game and the person wins (somebody

00:02:28.931 --> 00:02:33.220
wins) and goes out and jumps up and down and
they’re excited, the person I identify with

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:35.580
is not the tennis player.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:42.120
The person I identify with is the coach that
you get one second of who is just so proud

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:47.590
and feels so rewarded for what their player
had done.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.290
For me that is the most fulfilling experience.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:53.810
Being able to help others and being in the
position where somebody comes to me and says

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:54.810
“I need help.”

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:59.850
I think that is a highly privileged position
to be asked to provide help and in an area

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:00.850
in which I can help.

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:06.290
Film Courage: Have you seen people who have
the notion (whether it’s to be a great pianist

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:11.480
or to be a great writer) but in terms of the
day-to-day actions that they are going to

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.570
undergo, it’s actually not what they are
suited for?

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:15.570
Lee Jessup: Absolutely!

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:19.610
Film Courage: And what is that temperament
and what is that temperament…[such as] it’s

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:21.350
being alone, it’s being driven, it’s being
task-oriented.

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:22.350
Lee Jessup: It’s not just that.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:27.230
I know a lot of “writers” (and these I’ll
put that in quotation marks) who talk about

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.280
writing all the time.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.040
Who talk about what they want to write, how
much they want to be a writer and how much

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.510
they want to be in a room and how much….but…never
make time for the writing itself.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:42.251
So those are the writers who will usually
bring me to the conversation of “Do you

00:03:42.251 --> 00:03:43.820
really want to write?

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:47.890
Because you are talking about it all the time,
but you’re not actually doing it.”

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:54.900
And if you’re a writer who is not writing,
then what are you in terms of this career?

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.370
You have to have the discipline.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:58.480
You have to have the desire.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.280
You have to have the drive.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:04.610
And you have to be willing to make the sacrifice
that most writers do that it takes in order

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:05.860
to become a writer.

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:12.100
And that means waking up in the morning early
if you have a day job.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.880
Or writing on the weekends or writing after
hours rather than running around partying

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:16.880
with your friends.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.290
For most writers, there’s a lot of who are
not independently wealthy, right?

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:26.890
They are not going to be able to sit around
all day and take 4 hours a day to write and

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.920
go party and go run around and travel the
world.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.520
So most writers are going to find themselves
in a situation where they are going to have

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:33.630
a day job.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:38.850
Potentially they have a family, they have
a partner, they have friends, they have relationships

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:42.500
they want to maintain and so writing is going
to demand some level of sacrifice.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:48.820
I had a writer that I worked with for a number
of years who we would meet every 3 or 4 months

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.680
and every 3 or 4 months she would sit down
and tell me the list of excuses for why she

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:55.300
didn’t write the previous 3 or 4 months.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:05:01.110
So…the job was too hard and she had to work
out because she wasn’t healthy and she wasn’t

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:07.360
sleeping well and she had to move apartments…she
was fostering dogs and there was always something.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.450
And I truly believe that she wanted to be
a writer.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:16.030
But at the end of the day if you’re not
engaging in that thing, whether it’s writing

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:20.660
or playing piano (or any other thing) you
cannot claim the thing as your own.

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:23.900
You have to engage in it in some consistency.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:29.300
And similarly I meet writers who wrote a screenplay
ten years ago and haven't touched writing

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:30.300
since.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:31.300
They’re a writer.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:35.490
But you have to actively engage in the thing
if you want to be taken seriously as an owner

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:35.940
of the thing.

